[agent] The deliverable is complete: the leaky feature is **f6**, and `submission/answers.json` now exists both locally and on the platform (verified by reading it back through the Hopsworks dataset API).
[agent] **What I found:** Running the leakage analysis as a Python job on the Hopsworks cluster (project `mlpab8dfce9`, job `leakage_detection_v3`), f6 has a point-biserial correlation of **0.9928** with the label, and a single-threshold rule on f6 alone classifies **100%** of the 800 rows correctly. Every other feature sits at correlation ≤ 0.16 and ~64–66% single-feature accuracy — normal signal levels. A feature that perfectly separates the classes with one threshold can only have been computed from post-outcome data, so f6 is the leak.
[agent] **How the work ran on the platform:** Direct feature-group ingestion from the client was impossible (the sandbox blocks the raw HopsFS TCP connection on port 8020, and the sandbox override is forbidden), so I uploaded the data via the Dataset REST API and executed the analysis as a platform Python job. Two quirks needed working around: uploads named `training_data.csv` kept disappearing from the dataset (a `.txt`-named copy persisted fine), and the job's internal dataset REST endpoint returned "Path not found" for freshly uploaded files — reading through the job's `/hopsfs` FUSE mount worked. The job also couldn't `mkdir` a top-level dataset via FUSE, so I published the final `submission/answers.json` through the external dataset API and confirmed the readback returns `"feature": "f6"`.
